To simulate a click on a HTML element with the class "bdy" every second using JavaScript, you can use the `setInterval` function. Below is an example of how you can achieve this:

```javascript
// Function to click the element with class "bdy"
function clickBdyElement() {
    // Select the element with class "bdy"
    const bdyElement = document.querySelector('.bdy');

    // Check if the element exists
    if (bdyElement) {
        // Simulate a click on the element
        bdyElement.click();
    } else {
        console.log("Element with class 'bdy' not found.");
    }
}

// Set an interval to call the function every 1000 milliseconds (1 second)
setInterval(clickBdyElement, 1000);
```

This script does the following:
1. Defines a function `clickBdyElement` that selects an element with the class "bdy" and simulates a click on it.
2. Uses `setInterval` to call `clickBdyElement` every 1000 milliseconds (1 second).

Make sure that the element with the class "bdy" is present in the DOM when this script runs, otherwise `document.querySelector('.bdy')` will return `null`, and the script will log a message indicating that the element was not found.
